  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\n`CURLOPT_MAXLIFETIME_CONN` is not enforced when a new transfer is attached to an active multiplexed HTTP/2 connection. The connection-age eligibility check in `url_match_conn()` is gated behind `!CONN_INUSE(conn)`, so an in-use HTTP/2 connection with spare stream capacity is accepted without any age check. A new transfer silently reuses an over-age connection, bypassing certificate revalidation.\n\nSince 8.17.0 this option defaults to 24 hours and `libcurl-security.md` recommends it as the mechanism to bound how long a validated connection remains eligible: \"Further transfers require a new connection with validation performed again.\" The bypass defeats that guarantee for any multiplexed connection that still has an active stream.\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `lib/url.c`, `url_match_conn()` calls `Curl_cpool_conn_seems_healthy()` only for idle connections:\n\n```c\nif(!CONN_INUSE(conn) &&\n   !Curl_cpool_conn_seems_healthy(conn, m->data, &m->now)) {\n```\n\nAn active multiplexed connection passes `url_match_multiplex_limits()` (stream capacity check) and then falls through directly to `m->found = conn` without any health or age check.\n\nInside `Curl_cpool_conn_seems_healthy()` in `lib/conncache.c`, the age check is also guarded:\n\n```c\nif(!CONN_INUSE(conn) && cpool_conn_maxage(data, conn, pnow))\n  return FALSE;\n```\n\nSo the creation-age check (`conn_max_age_ms`) never evaluates for an in-use multiplexed connection.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nTested at commit `c2676bf9e639e31e44b12a2cc82cf2046231b056` with OpenSSL and nghttp2. All files below are self-contained in a Docker build.\n\n```\ndocker build -t curl-maxlifetime-h2-poc .\ndocker run --rm curl-maxlifetime-h2-poc\n```\n\nThe PoC does:\n\n1. Generate CA A, unrelated CA B, and a localhost server cert signed by CA A.\n2. Start an HTTP/2 TLS server on port 8443.\n3. Transfer A opens an HTTP/2 stream using CA A. The server holds this stream open (sends headers but no end-of-stream), keeping the connection active.\n4. Wait 2.2 seconds (CURLOPT_MAXLIFETIME_CONN is set to 1 second).\n5. Replace the CA file on disk with CA B (same path). TLS session cache and CA cache are disabled.\n6. Transfer B is added normally. It succeeds on the same connection (conn_id=0, num_connects=0) — the age check was skipped.\n7. Transfer C is added with CURLOPT_FRESH_CONNECT=1 as a control. It opens a new connection and fails with CURLE_PEER_FAILED_VERIFICATION (60), proving the CA replacement is effective.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nApply the creation-age eligibility check before attaching any matching connection, including active multiplexed ones. An over-age active connection should be rejected for the new transfer and marked non-reusable, while allowing its existing streams to finish normally.\n\n## Affected versions\n7.80.0 through 8.21.0+ (current master). The severity increased in 8.17.0 when the option became enabled by default (24-hour lifetime).\n\n## Impact\n\n## Summary:\nA new transfer can silently reuse an HTTP/2 connection that has exceeded its configured maximum lifetime, bypassing the certificate revalidation that would occur on a fresh connection.",
